Design and Analysis A. Difference between prefix and postfix increment Prefix ++i Postfix i++ Increments first Returns the old value first Returns a reference Returns a copy Does not require an extra temporary object Creates a temporary copy Generally preferred for class objects Used when the old value is required
The compiler distinguishes them using the dummy int parameter. Time& operator++(); // Prefix Time operator++(int); // Postfix

B. Why does postfix return a copy?

Postfix increment must preserve the original value before incrementing. Therefore, it creates a temporary copy and returns that old value.

C. Why are two versions of operator[] required?

Non-const operator[] allows modification of a playlist track.
Const operator[] allows reading tracks from a constant Playlist object without modifying it. D. What happens when an index is out of range?
The program checks whether the index is negative or greater than or equal to the playlist size. If invalid, it throws std::out_of_range instead of accessing invalid memory. 6. Experimental Investigation Experiment 1: Prefix and postfix values Given: Time t(0, 0, 5);

